    },
    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: '28 dominoes',
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '28 dominoes is block or draw dominoes with a double-6 set of 28 tiles. Two to five players; 7 tiles each (2-3 players) or 5 (4-5 players). Highest double leads. Match numbers on open ends; doubles placed crosswise. Block: pass if cannot play. Draw: draw from boneyard until you can play. First out wins; or lowest pips if blocked.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Domino tiles originated in China in the 12th–13th centuries; the modern 28-tile double-six block and draw games developed in France and spread across Europe and worldwide.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Two or more players.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Double-6 domino set of 28 tiles: each tile has two ends with 0–6 pips; every combination appears once. Used for block and draw games.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Double-6 set, table',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '7 each (2-3 players) or 5 each (4-5); rest boneyard.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'Block dominoes: Standard blocking game with a double-six set: each player draws an initial hand, plays matching tiles to the open ends, and passes when unable to play; the winner is first to play all tiles or the player with the lowest pip total when the game is blocked.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: "Draw dominoes: Draw variant using the same double-six set: when a player cannot play they must draw from the boneyard until they find a playable tile or the stock is empty; scoring still uses the remaining pips in opponents' hands.",
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
